in 2016 this was a very confusing place  to be designing streets is a little bit  like being a detective if you look hard  enough you can see the traces of all the  decisions people made before claire is  the principal in charge of wxy an  architecture firm with a focus on urban  design planning and community-based  architecture i'm going to walk you  through three street redesigns that wxy  has done in new york city  sometimes you hear the term bad street  there's no vegetation there's nothing to  look at there's nothing to visit  generally people will go oh my god  that's not a street i want to walk down  and therefore it's not a great street  and the opportunity as an architect or  landscape architect or urban designer is  to kind of see what's happening really  listen and  observe because there needs to be design  solutions to the problems that people  are experiencing on their streets let's  talk about cooper union cooper union is  an intersection in new york where bowery  splits off into cooper square the  original street only  did this  the bowery was widened and there were  too many lanes for cars and not enough  crosswalks for pedestrians what's really  interesting is that departments of  transportation were actually departments  of cars for a long time and mostly they  would describe streets by the number of  lanes the street ultimately expanded to  four lanes of traffic which resulted in  a very confusing intersection we're  seeing everything being two ways two  moving lanes in every direction but  confusion in the middle and here's our  sidewalk that is a lot of road bed  because if i was looking at just dotting  in you know what a street would be right  i'm left with a kind of no-man zone here  for pedestrians there's no clear way to  cross the intersection so the way people  have tried to deal with it is where you  see all sorts of painted medians all  over the place to try and kind of tell  people what they should be doing and it  was completely car focused which didn't  add much to the character of the street  the bigger you make roads the more it  attracts traffic but it doesn't really  attract things like business more  cyclists more walking in a lot of ways  it's a kind of a human behavior issue  the solution reduce the number of lanes  add pedestrian crosswalks and limit the  direction of traffic instead of a bunch  of streets  you have a very straightforward way for  cars even which they could not do before  to drive up the street which is now one  way  and what that's done is it's made more  sidewalks and that's really  fundamentally what you're seeing with  street design is a way to make streets  still serve cars efficiently but also  make it easier for people on buses  people biking all of these  kinds of lanes can kind of shift  priorities and give new spaces now let's  head to brooklyn to take a look at alby  square albe square is an intersection in  downtown brooklyn where fleet fulton  street alby square west and bond street  all meet up but many of the subways are  sort of off to the corner on very narrow  sidewalks this area has a lot of  pedestrian traffic but not a lot of  space to maneuver especially since these  are small historical streets when you  have historic streets they're not always  as wide as connector streets that got  cleared out because people wanted more  lanes so a lot of the planning that's  happening around albe square in these  remnants of different grids that make up  downtown brooklyn and all the new  buildings is to create more room  what do we see on the right hand side  well all of a sudden this street right  here isn't there anymore but now this  street  meets  we have a much larger public space  instead of an island surrounded by  streets we have an island that's now  attached to  the sidewalk for  a beautiful bank building  and a new mall called alby square mall  that's full of activity next up is astor  place we're at pretty well well-known  destination in new york called astor  place esther place is an intersection  where lafayette 8th street 4th avenue  and cooper square all converge these are  streets that are doing a lot of work in  2016 this was a very confusing place to  be wide streets narrow streets  there's a lot of buses hanging out  waiting so people just walked in the  street that's the subway and there's  a number of kind of geometries of  streets  all cutting through it that is called  the almo the people have nicknamed the  cube you see people hang out underneath  it the cube is kind of sitting on also  kind of a floating concrete median and  then we have a street  here  known as lafayette street that is going  uptown in addition to these main streets  astor place has a number of historical  streets all feeding into the traffic  flow this where i'm drawing this dotted  line is one of the oldest streets in new  york and it got renamed stuyvesant  street this street must have started as  a dirt path and in fact a lot of streets  in new york center street lafayette  broadway were actually just trails and  then they got widened they got paved  these multifunctional streets home to  art installation subway stops and  historical remnants of old new york are  prone to the wear and tear of high  traffic areas this is not a low space  but there's a lot of flooding that  happens if rain comes really really  quickly  what you see now today not only is there  more trees but there's actually bio  swales and green infrastructure there's  more bike racks there's bigger sidewalks  you have a huge number of additional  green  spaces and trees there's something  called the curb line and the art i think  of street making is figuring out where  the curb goes so that all the buildings  look and feel even better if you put the  curb too close to a building or in the  wrong alignment the whole street feels  off so it's sort of like a great room or  how clothes fit  where the curb is is kind of like the  tailoring of streets  we can start  thinking about streets kind of like air  and water as the  thing that we share there are private  streets but in general successful  important streets are shared by just a  multiplicity of players right and even  by walking so streets in a sense are the  design problem of today to accommodate  getting more out of them and making them  attractive to people but they're also  the design problem of the near future  [Music]
 

